<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Middle Francia</h1><p class="st-meta">843 CE – 855 CE · capital Aachen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Central share of the Carolingian empire, stretching from Frisia to Rome, given to the emperor Lothair I in 843.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 ended the war between the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. The eldest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a>, kept the imperial title and received a long strip of land between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-francia">West Francia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-francia">East Francia</a>, including the imperial cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It held the richest heartlands of the Carolingian dynasty but had no common identity, language or natural frontiers, and suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attacks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and Arab raids in the south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Shortly before his death in 855 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> retired to the abbey of Prüm and, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-prum">Treaty of Prüm</a>, divided the kingdom among his three sons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy">Louis II</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> the north, which became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lotharingia">Kingdom of Lotharingia</a>, and Charles the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-provence">Kingdom of Provence</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/middle-francia.jpg" alt="Middle Francia" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: maix¿?; derivative work by Alphathon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_Francia_843.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>843 CE – 855 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aachen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lothair I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Old Frankish, Romance</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Lothair I, Francia Media</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=849&amp;focus=middle-francia" aria-label="Middle Francia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Middle Francia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5139 672L5139 672M5139 672L5139 672M5139 672L5144 875M5139 672L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5139 672h0M5139 672h0M5144 875h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5139 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5139 672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aachen. <a href="/atlas?year=849&amp;focus=middle-francia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
